# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Rerror tag[2] ename[s]
```

# DESCRIPTION

The `Rerror` message, type 107, returns a string describing why a request failed.
It replaces the reply the request would have had, and its <tag> is the request's.
There is no `Terror`: type 106 is no message, and a server sent one hangs up.

A VectraOS server's operations fail with a `vx_status` (see intro(2)), and the server turns it into Plan 9's wording where Plan 9 has one:

| Status | <ename> |
| `VX_ERR_NOT_FOUND` | `file does not exist` |
| `VX_ERR_EXISTS` | `file already exists` |
| `VX_ERR_ACCESS` | `permission denied` |
| `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE` | `unknown fid` |
| `VX_ERR_BAD_STATE` | `fid already in use` |
| `VX_ERR_RANGE` | `offset out of range` |
| `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY` | `out of memory` |
| `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED` | `operation not supported` |
| `VX_ERR_TOO_SMALL` | `message too large for msize` |
| `VX_ERR_REFUSED` | `connection refused` |
| `VX_ERR_TIMED_OUT` | `connection timed out` |
| `VX_ERR_PEER_CLOSED` | `i/o on hungup channel` |
| `VX_ERR_INTERRUPTED` | `interrupted` |
| `VX_ERR_NO_CHILD` | `no living children` |
| `VX_ERR_IO` | `i/o error` |
| `VX_ERR_NO_SPACE` | `file system full` |
| `VX_ERR_INVALID` | `bad message` |

Any other status, such as `VX_ERR_KILLED`, is sent as `i/o error`.
Servers, svcd(8) and vx-ns print a status with the same words, so a message printed from a status reads as the error that came over the wire.

VectraOS's client turns <ename> back into the status, ignoring ASCII case.
It also knows the wordings of Unix's `strerror` and of u9fs, as a Unix 9P server passes them on, though it never sends them:

| <ename> heard | Status |
| `no such file or directory`, `not a directory` | `VX_ERR_NOT_FOUND` |
| `file exists`, `file or directory already exists` | `VX_ERR_EXISTS` |
| `read-only file system`, `is a directory`, `operation not permitted` | `VX_ERR_ACCESS` |
| `fid unknown or out of range` | `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE` |
| `no space left on device` | `VX_ERR_NO_SPACE` |

Any other <ename> becomes `VX_ERR_INVALID`.
The program sees only the status; the text itself goes no further than the client.

Every 9Px session's errors are `Rerror` strings, whatever its extensions: 9P2000.L's `Rlerror`, type 7, which carries an errno, is no 9Px message, and since a 9P2000.L client is answered with plain 9P2000 (see version(5)), it never expects one.

A request too broken to answer gets no `Rerror`: one that does not decode exactly (a <size> that is not the message's, a field that runs past it, bytes after the last field, a string holding NUL, a walk of more than 16 names), an R-message sent to a server, and a message of a type 9Px does not have make the server hang up the connection.
So does a reply the transport cannot carry.

